      "description": "Validate a US dollar amount. Rules:\n- It must start with a dollar sign immediately followed by a numeric amount.\n- The numeric part may be an integer (no decimal part) or a decimal with exactly two digits after a decimal point.\n- If the integer part is present and has more than one digit, it must not start with zero.\n- The integer part may be omitted only if the decimal part is present, in which case the decimal point may be preceded by zero (e.g., $0.99) or omitted (e.g., $.99).\n- If the integer part is zero, the decimal part is optional (e.g., $0 or $0.00).\n- A comma may be used as a thousands separator in the integer part, but only if placed correctly: every group of three digits after the first group must be preceded by a comma, and the first group must be one to three digits without leading zeros.\n- No other characters are allowed.",
      "regex": "/^\\$(?:(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,2}(?:,\\d{3})*)(?:\\.\\d{2})?|\\.\\d{2})$/"
