      "description": "Match a euro price string in French formatting.\n\n- The string must consist of an amount followed by a non-breaking space (U+00A0) and then the euro sign (€).\n- The amount uses a comma as the decimal separator and may include thousands separators (spaces) in groups of three digits, starting from the right of the integer part.\n- The integer part must have at least one digit and must not start with a zero unless it is exactly zero.\n- The fractional part, if present, must have exactly two digits.\n- Thousands separators are optional; if present, they must be regular spaces (U+0020).\n- Leading and trailing spaces are not allowed.\n- No currency symbols other than € are permitted.\n\nExamples of valid strings: \"1 234,56 €\", \"0,99 €\", \"12 345 678,90 €\".\nExamples of invalid strings: \"1234,56€\", \"1.234,56 €\", \"12,345.67 €\", \"1234,5 €\", \"1 234,56 €\".\n\nWrite a JavaScript regular expression that matches exactly these strings.",
      "regex": "/^(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,2}(?: \\d{3})*)(?:,\\d{2})?\\u00a0€$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 2,
      "description": "We need to validate that a price field contains a monetary amount with exactly two decimal places (like a typical dollar amount). The amount can be a whole number with no decimal part, or it can have a decimal point followed by exactly two digits. No thousands separators, no currency symbol, no negative sign. Just a plain number.",
      "regex": "/^\\d+(?:\\.\\d{2})?$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 3,
      "description": "In our accounting ledger, negative amounts are written inside parentheses, like (123.45). We need a validation pattern for such negative amounts. The format should be: an opening parenthesis, then an optional dollar sign, then one or more digits, optionally followed by a decimal point and exactly two digits, then a closing parenthesis. No commas or spaces allowed. Positive amounts are handled elsewhere, so this pattern should only match the negative format described.",
      "regex": "/^\\(\\$?\\d+(?:\\.\\d{2})?\\)$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 4,
      "description": "Match an amount in Japanese yen. The amount is written with digits only, no decimal part, and may include comma thousands separators. The string must end with the yen sign (¥). For example, ¥1000, ¥1,234,567, and ¥0 are valid, but ¥100.50, 1000¥, and ¥1,00 are not.",
      "regex": "/^(?:\\d{1,3}(?:,\\d{3})*|\\d+)¥$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 5,
      "description": "Validate a US dollar amount string.\n\nRules:\n1. The string must start with a dollar sign ($).\n2. The dollar sign is followed by an integer part with one or more digits.\n3. The integer part may include commas as thousands separators. If commas are used, they must separate groups of exactly three digits, and the first group must have one to three digits. Leading zeros are allowed only as a single zero (e.g., \"0\" is valid, but \"00\" is not).\n4. The integer part may be followed by an optional decimal part consisting of a dot and exactly two digits.\n5. No other characters are allowed anywhere in the string.\n6. The entire string must match from start to end.\n\nExamples of valid strings: $1, $123, $1,234, $1,234.56, $0.99, $0, $1,000,000.00\nExamples of invalid strings: $1,23, $1,234.5, $1.234, $1,234, $0123, $1,234.567",
      "regex": "/^\\$(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,2}(?:,\\d{3})*)(?:\\.\\d{2})?$/"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "slot": 1,
      "description": "An API key must begin with the prefix 'sk_' followed by exactly 32 lowercase letters or digits, and nothing else.",
      "regex": "/^sk_[a-z0-9]{32}$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 2,
      "description": "The signup form requires a password that is at least 8 characters long, contains at least one lowercase letter, one uppercase letter, and one digit. Only letters and digits are allowed.",
      "regex": "/^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*[0-9])[A-Za-z0-9]{8,}$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 3,
      "description": "password must not contain the word password anywhere, case-insensitive",
      "regex": "/^(?:(?!password).)*$/i"
    },
    {
      "slot": 4,
      "description": "A valid password is at least 10 characters long and includes at least one lowercase letter, one uppercase letter, one digit, and one of these symbols: !@#$%^&*. No other characters are allowed.",
      "regex": "/^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*[0-9])(?=.*[!@#$%^&*])[A-Za-z0-9!@#$%^&*]{10,}$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 5,
      "description": "A PIN code must be exactly 4 to 6 digits, but it cannot consist of a single repeated digit.",
      "regex": "/^(?!([0-9])\\1+$)[0-9]{4,6}$/"
    }
  ]
}

{"items":[{"slot": 1, "description": "Our upload widget asks users to name the report file they are attaching. Marketing wants to make sure people don't type characters that would break the file system on our servers, so for that name field we accept any name made of letters, digits, underscores and hyphens, with a dot allowed only before the extension, and the extension itself must be letters.", "regex": "/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\.[A-Za-z]+$/", "difficulty": "simple", "register": "business"}, {"slot": 2, "description": "A test file name is the base name of the module followed by either .spec or .test and then .ts or .js. The base name may contain letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens but must not be empty.", "regex": "/^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+\\.(?:spec|test)\\.(?:ts|js)$/", "difficulty": "medium", "register": "sentence"}, {"slot": 3, "description": "Validate an image file name:
- must have a non-empty base name;
- the base name may contain letters, digits, spaces, underscores, hyphens and parentheses;
- the extension is one of jpg, jpeg, png, gif, bmp, webp;
- the extension comparison is case-insensitive.", "regex": "/^[A-Za-z0-9 _()-]+\.(?:jpe?g|png|gif|bmp|webp)$/i", "difficulty": "medium", "register": "spec"}, {"slot": 4, "description": "windows abs path drive letter backslash separated segments no empty segments no <>:"|?* in segments optional trailing backslash", "regex": "/^[A-Za-z]:\\(?:[^\\<>:"|?*\r\n]+\\)*[^\\<>:"|?*\r\n]*\\?$/", "difficulty": "hard", "register": "terse"}, {"slot": 5, "description": "In the desktop app's file picker history we store paths exactly as the user typed them. We only want to keep entries that are absolute Windows paths, so any path that starts with a drive letter followed by a colon and a backslash is fine, but it must not contain any of the characters Windows forbids in a path segment.", "regex": "/^[A-Za-z]:\\(?:[^\\<>:"|?*\r\n]+\\)*[^\\<>:"|?*\r\n]*$/", "difficulty": "medium", "register": "business"}]}

