      "description": "Validate an HTTP status line as a string. The line must begin with 'HTTP/' followed by a version consisting of one or more digits, a dot, and one or more digits. Then a space, followed by exactly three digits (the status code). The status code must start with 4 or 5 (i.e., represent a 4xx or 5xx code). After the status code, a space, then a reason phrase of one or more characters (any except newline). No leading or trailing whitespace is allowed.",
      "regex": "/^HTTP\\/[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+ [45][0-9]{2} .+$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 2,
      "description": "Our server writes log lines that always start with a severity level: one of the uppercase words ERROR, WARN, INFO, or DEBUG, immediately followed by a colon and a space. The rest of the line is a non-empty message that may contain any characters except newline. We need to check if a given log line matches this format exactly from beginning to end.",
      "regex": "/^(ERROR|WARN|INFO|DEBUG): .+$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 3,
      "description": "Match a single line from an Apache or nginx access log. The line starts with the client IP address: four dot-separated decimal numbers, each between 0 and 255, with no leading zeros unless the number is exactly 0. Then a space, a hyphen, a space, a hyphen, a space, and a timestamp enclosed in square brackets. The timestamp format is dd/MMM/yyyy:HH:mm:ss Z, where dd is two digits, MMM is the three-letter English month abbreviation (capitalized), yyyy is four digits, HH, mm, ss are two digits, and Z is a timezone offset like +0000 or -0700. After the timestamp and a closing bracket, a space, a double-quoted HTTP request line (e.g., \"GET /index.html HTTP/1.1\"), then a space, a three-digit status code, a space, and a size in bytes (one or more digits, or a hyphen if unknown). No extra spaces or characters are allowed before or after.",
      "regex": "/^(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2})\\.(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2})\\.(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2})\\.(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2}) - - \\[[0-9]{2}\\/(Jan|Feb|Mar|Apr|May|Jun|Jul|Aug|Sep|Oct|Nov|Dec)\\/[0-9]{4}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2} [+-][0-9]{4}\\] \"[^\"]*\" [0-9]{3} ([0-9]+|-)$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 4,
      "description": "Match a log line that contains an error code anywhere in it. The error code consists of the uppercase letters 'ERR' followed by exactly three digits. The line must contain at least one such code, but the code may be preceded or followed by any other characters (including spaces, punctuation, etc.) except that the line cannot contain newline characters. The entire string is not required to be only the error code; we just need to know if any error code is present.",
      "regex": "/^.*ERR[0-9]{3}.*$/"
    },
    {
      "slot": 5,
      "description": "We receive access logs from our web servers and need to validate each line before storing it. The expected format is the common log format: an IP address (four dot-separated numbers, each 0-255), then a space, a hyphen, a space, a hyphen, a space, a date-time in square brackets in the format day/month/year:hour:minute:second zone, where day is two digits, month is two digits, year is four digits, hour, minute, second are two digits each, and zone is a plus or minus followed by four digits. After that, a space, a quoted HTTP request line (anything inside double quotes), a space, a three-digit status code, a space, and the response size in bytes (a number or a hyphen). No extra characters before or after.",
      "regex": "/^(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2})\\.(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2})\\.(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2})\\.(25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1?[0-9]{1,2}) - - \\[[0-9]{2}\\/[0-9]{2}\\/[0-9]{4}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2} [+-][0-9]{4}\\] \"[^\"]*\" [0-9]{3} ([0-9]+|-)$/"
    }
  ]
}

{
